{"repositoryId":1103294465,"availability":"available","full":"silverlila/slider","name":"slider","description":"A slider and carousel library where the browser's own scrolling moves the slides.","category":"Other","language":"TypeScript","stars":0,"forks":0,"created":"2025-11-24T17:20:31Z","updated":"2026-10-16T17:32:41Z","license":"MIT","branch":"main","homepage":"https://silverlila.github.io/slider/","topics":[],"demo":"https://silverlila.github.io/slider/","color":"#193546","ink":"#a6d6e8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-19T06:19:58.985Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-19T06:19:58.985Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-19T06:19:58.985Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-19T06:19:58.985Z","stars":0,"forks":0}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["A slider and carousel library where the browser's own scrolling moves the slides.","The slides sit in an ordinary scroll container with CSS scroll-snap. JavaScript measures the layout, tracks which slide is active, drives the buttons and dots, and adds what the platform lacks: mouse dragging, an infinite loop, autoplay, per-slide effect values and thumbnail sync. Because the scroll is real, trackpads, mouse wheels, keyboard arrows, touch momentum, find-in-page and focus scrolling all behave the way they do everywhere else on the page, and the layout is already correct before JavaScript loads.","Scrolling, drag and animation come from use-scroller. The package has two entry points:"],"features":["@silverlila/slider/core: framework-free. No React anywhere in its import graph.","@silverlila/slider: React components and hooks. It also re-exports everything in core."],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/silverlila/slider/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"e3181a448393b839686e85fb89de645078ad83607f1794d648734560e5ed2b3b","extractedAt":"2026-10-19T06:19:59.107Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-19T06:19:59.107Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"A slider and carousel library where the browser's own scrollin